    { "provider": "anthropic", "page": "https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/about-claude/pricing", "read_on": "2026-10-27" },
    { "provider": "openai", "page": "https://developers.openai.com/api/docs/pricing", "read_on": "2026-10-27" },
    { "provider": "openai", "page": "https://developers.openai.com/api/docs/guides/prompt-caching", "read_on": "2026-10-27" },
    { "provider": "google", "page": "https://ai.google.dev/gemini-api/docs/pricing", "read_on": "2026-10-27" }
  ],
  "notes": [
    "Every rate is the page's own number, except the fast-mode cache rates. The page says the prompt-caching multipliers apply on top of fast-mode pricing, and gives them: a 5-minute write is 1.25 times input, a 1-hour write 2 times, and a cache read 0.05 times on Opus 5.5 and 0.1 times on Opus 5 and Opus 4.8.",
    "A tier's limit is a prompt length, which counts all of a call's input tokens: input, cache reads and cache writes. Each call is priced on its own. OpenAI's long-context rates start above 272,000 input tokens, and Google's above 200,000.",
    "The anthropic rows are list prices for global inference on Anthropic's first-party API. US-only inference costs 1.1 times as much on Claude 4.6 and later, and the records do not say which a call used. Claude in Microsoft Foundry bills at the same per-model rates, so the provider foundry reads the anthropic rows (aliases).",
    "Amazon Bedrock and Google Cloud set their own Claude prices, and their pages give them only to a browser, so neither has rows yet and their records carry no cost. On both, regional and multi-region endpoints cost 10 percent more than global ones, for Claude 4.5 and later, as Anthropic's page says.",
    "The openai rows are the Standard tier. GPT-5.6 and later bill a cache write at 1.25 times input for the default 30-minute lifetime, and a 12-hour write at twice that. Codex reports one cache-write count, so a write is priced at the 30-minute rate. Earlier models bill no separate cache write, so their rows have no cache-write rate. The Pro models have no cached-input price on the page, and no row. The Fast, Flex and Batch rates are not used, because the records do not say which a call used.",
    "The google rows are the paid Standard tier, for text input. Audio input costs more, and the storage of a context cache is billed by the hour, which no record holds. The rows hold the rates in force on 2026-10-27: gemini-4-argon's rates double on 2026-12-01, and gemini-3.8-flash's and gemini-3.6-flash's on 2027-01-01.",
    "A row with no cache-write rate prices a cache write at its input rate, as OpenAI and Google bill one.",
    "A model with no row here gets no cost, never a guessed one."
  ],
